@page "/docs/registry"
<PageTitle>Registry - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Registry</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">community</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">static JSON</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground">A registry is how Blaizio distributes source: a set of JSON files any static host can serve. The official components come from one; you can run your own to share components, enhanced variants of the built-ins, or themes - and consumers install from it with the same <code>blaizio add</code> they already use. This page covers the format, the authoring workflow, and how to get listed on the <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="community">community page</a>.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How it works</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">A registry is two layers of JSON. The <b>manifest</b> (<code>registry.json</code>) is the source of truth you edit: it names each item and points at its files on disk. The <b>built output</b> is what you host: one <code>&lt;item&gt;.json</code> per item with every file's contents inlined, plus an <code>index.json</code> catalogue. <code>blaizio build</code> turns the former into the latter. The CLI resolves an item by fetching <code>&lt;registry-url&gt;/&lt;name&gt;.json</code> - no server code, no database; GitHub Pages or any static bucket is enough.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The manifest</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">One item per installable thing. <code>registryDependencies</code> pulls sibling items in automatically; <code>nugetDependencies</code> runs <code>dotnet add package</code> on install. A minimal manifest with one component and one theme:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/RegistryJson" />
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b bg-muted/50">
                        <th class="px-3 py-2 text-start font-medium">Item type</th>
                        <th class="px-3 py-2 text-start font-medium">Installs</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <tr class="border-b"><td class="px-3 py-2 whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry:ui</code></td><td class="px-3 py-2">A styled component - its files are copied into the consumer's output folder and rewritten to their namespace.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b"><td class="px-3 py-2 whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry:lib</code></td><td class="px-3 py-2">Shared helper files other items depend on (utilities, extensions).</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b"><td class="px-3 py-2 whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry:theme</code></td><td class="px-3 py-2">Token overrides. No files - a <code>cssVars</code> payload with <code>light</code> and <code>dark</code> maps, patched into the consumer's tokens file (<code>:root</code> / <code>.dark</code>).</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b"><td class="px-3 py-2 whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry:font</code></td><td class="px-3 py-2">A font selection (heading or body) applied through the font overlay.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="px-3 py-2 whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry:template</code></td><td class="px-3 py-2">A full project template, scaffolded by <code>blaizio new</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Each file entry carries a <code>path</code> (relative to the manifest), a <code>type</code>, and optionally a <code>target</code> to override where it lands relative to the consumer's output folder. Token names in <code>cssVars</code> may be written with or without the <code>--</code> prefix.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Authoring components</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Write your components under the <code>Blaizio.Ui</code> root namespace - the same convention the official registry uses. On install the CLI rewrites that prefix to the consumer's namespace (<code>MyApp.Components.Ui.&lt;YourRegistry&gt;</code>), so cross-references between your components keep compiling. Reference the headless layer (<code>Blaizio.Base</code>, <code>Blaizio.Icons</code>) as NuGet dependencies; never copy Base sources into a registry.</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">For a tree of components, <code>blaizio generate</code> writes the manifest for you: one item per component folder, cross-component dependencies inferred from the <code>Bz*</code> types each file references. Then validate and build:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/BuildValidate" />
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Host the output directory anywhere static. A GitHub Pages workflow that runs those two commands on push is the whole pipeline.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Authoring themes</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">A theme item is data, not files: <code>cssVars.light</code> patches the consumer's <code>:root</code> block, <code>cssVars.dark</code> their <code>.dark</code> block, declaration by declaration - everything else in their tokens file survives. The fastest way to produce the values is the <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="themes">/create</a> composer: dial in a look, copy the theme CSS, translate the changed tokens into the two maps. Installing is <code>blaizio add @("@you")/your-theme</code>; going back is <code>blaizio apply &lt;preset&gt;</code> (the recorded preset re-applies its own values over yours).</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Check contrast before you publish: <CmdChip Command="blaizio contrast" /> audits a project's tokens file against WCAG AA in both modes.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Consuming a registry</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Consumers record your registry once under a namespace, then use <code>@("@namespace/item")</code> references everywhere:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/ConsumerAdd" />
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Namespaced installs stay out of the way of everything else: files land under their own folder (<code>Components/Ui/Acme/</code>), components get their own namespace segment (<code>MyApp.Components.Ui.Acme</code>), and <code>blaizio.json</code> records them as <code>@("@acme/tag")</code> - so your <code>tag</code> and another registry's <code>tag</code> coexist, and <code>update</code>, <code>add --diff</code> and <code>remove</code> all work per registry.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">A word on trust</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Installing from a registry copies source code into the consumer's project and installs the NuGet packages the item declares - that code compiles and runs as part of their app. Only add registries you trust, and inspect unfamiliar items first: <code>blaizio view @("@ns")/item</code> prints every file without writing anything, and <code>blaizio add --dry-run</code> shows the full plan. Community registries listed on this site are maintained by their authors and are not audited by Blaizio.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Get listed</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">The <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="community">community page</a> is driven by two JSON files in the Blaizio repository. Your registry's content stays on YOUR host - the listing is just a pointer. Submitting is a normal GitHub pull request:</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 ps-6 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            <li>Fork <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="https://github.com/blaizio/blaizio" target="_blank" rel="noopener">blaizio/blaizio</a> (or use GitHub's pencil button on the file - it forks for you).</li>
            <li>Add your entry to the right file:
                <ul class="mt-1.5 list-disc space-y-1.5 ps-5">
                    <li><b>A registry</b>: <code>docs/Blaizio.Docs/wwwroot/community/registries.json</code> - your <code>@("@namespace")</code>, homepage, registry URL and a one-line description.</li>
                    <li><b>A theme</b>: <code>docs/Blaizio.Docs/wwwroot/community/themes.json</code> - the same <code>light</code>/<code>dark</code> maps a theme item carries, plus a title and your handle. Themes carry their full data in the entry, so they become applyable and copyable on the community page directly with nothing to host:</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ol>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/CommunityThemeEntry" />
        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 ps-6 text-sm text-muted-foreground" start="3">
            <li>Open the pull request. For registries, check the manifest passes <code>blaizio registry validate</code> and the URL is publicly reachable - review checks both before merging.</li>
            <li>Once merged, the next docs deployment shows your entry on <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="community">/community</a>.</li>
        </ol>
    </section>
</div>
